'''Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Barmak''' was one of the [[Barmakids]], a family of [[Iranian peoples|Iranian]] functionaries who rose to great power under the [[Abbasid]] caliph [[Harun al-Rashid]] ({{reign|786|809}}).

==Life==
He was the brother of [[Yahya ibn Khalid]],{{sfn|Barthold|Sourdel|1960|p=1036}} whose appointment as [[vizier (Abbasid Caliphate)|vizier]] by Caliph [[Harun al-Rashid]] in 786 began the family's twenty-year domination of the Abbasid Caliphate's public affairs.{{sfn|Barthold|Sourdel|1960|p=1034}} Muhammad served as the Caliph's chamberlain ({{Transliteration|ar|[[hajib]]}}) from 788 to 795.{{sfn|Barthold|Sourdel|1960|p=1036}} He was replaced by [[al-Fadl ibn al-Rabi]],{{sfn|Bosworth|1989|pp=152–153}} who in the anecdotes of the time appears both as the Barmakids' foil and main antagonist at court.{{sfn|Kennedy|2006|pp=33, 79}}

Muhammad also served Harun al-Rashid as governor in the [[Yemen]] and the [[Jazira (caliphal province)|Jazira]].{{sfn|Crone|1980|p=176}}

He was the only Barmakid to be spared during the family's abrupt fall in 803,{{sfn|Barthold|Sourdel|1960|p=1036}}{{sfn|van Bladel|2012}}{{sfn|Bosworth|1989|pp=219, 222, 224}} likely because he had reported on the attempted flight of the [[Alid]] [[Yahya ibn Abdallah]], who had been released by Yahya's son [[Ja'far ibn Yahya|Ja'far]], contrary to the Caliph's orders to have him killed.{{sfn|Madelung|2002|p=243}}

